Uhunmwu-Ekue, hip or pendant mask
Royal Guild of Brass Casters (Igun Eronmwon)
Nigeria, Kingdom of Benin, Edo; 17th/18th century; brass
Museum Rietberg, 2011.9
Purchased with funds from Regula Brunner-Vontobel
https://rietberg.ch/en/collections/2011-9 (https://rietberg.ch/en/collections/2011-9)
The pendant mask was part of the ceremonial clothing of an Oba or high-ranking dignitary of the palace. During ceremonies, the pendant was worn over a wrap on the left hip or around the neck. The mask wears a crown and a headband made of coral beads. The lower part is surrounded by a symbol of the rising sun, called *Owen I bae de ku*, "the sun misses no day".
Provenance
17th/18th century Royal Guild of Brass Casters at the Court of Benin; 
1897 Looted by the British Army;
(...); 
2010 Purchased by Jacques Germain, Montreal; 
2011 Purchased by the Museum Rietberg, Zurich; 
CC-BY Nicolas Rolle/Museum Rietberg/Patrik Fuchs
